    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2x + 6y &=2 \\\\ -12x - 4y &=12\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $x$ from the system:\n\nTo eliminate $x$, multiply the\n{{field-1}}\nequation by\n{{field-2}},\nand add the equations.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Notice that the coefficient of $x$ in the first equation is $-2$, while in the second equation it is $-12$.\n\nIf we multiply the first equation by $-6$, then the coefficient of $x$ will turn into $12$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $-12$ in the second equation.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2x + 6y &=2 && \\times (-6) \\\\ -12x - 4y &=12\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis gives the equivalent system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}12x - 36y &=-12 \\\\ -12x - 4y &=12\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $x$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cancel{12x} - 36y &= -12 \\\\\n\\cancel{-12x} - 4y &= 12 \\\\\n(-36y - 4y) &= (-12 + 12) \\\\\n-40y &= 0 \\\\\ny &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n*Multiply the $\\text{first}$ equation by $-6$, and add the equations*.",
