    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2s - 3t &=8 \\\\ -6s - 7t &=40\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $s$ from the system:\n\nTo eliminate $s$, multiply the\n{{field-1}}\nequation by\n{{field-2}},\nand add the equations.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Notice that the coefficient of $s$ in the first equation is $2$, while in the second equation it is $-6$.\n\nIf we multiply the first equation by $3$, then the coefficient of $s$ will turn into $6$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $-6$ in the second equation.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2s - 3t &=8 && \\times (3) \\\\ -6s - 7t &=40\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis gives the equivalent system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6s - 9t &=24 \\\\ -6s - 7t &=40\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $s$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cancel{6s} - 9t &= 24 \\\\\n\\cancel{-6s} - 7t &= 40 \\\\\n(-9t - 7t) &= (24 + 40) \\\\\n-16t &= 64 \\\\\nt &= -4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n*Multiply the $\\text{first}$ equation by $3$, and add the equations*.",
